      "text": "Ghanaian Pidgin English is part of the West African Pidgin English continuum, which includes the varieties spoken in Sierra Leone (the creole Krio), Ghana, Nigeria, Bioko and Cameroon.1 The many similarities between the restructured Englishes spoken in these countries can to a large part be explained by the fact that the Ghanaian, Nigerian, and Cameroonian varieties are descendants of Krio (see below and Huber 1999a: 75–134 for details)."
     }
    ],
